Here is the ruthless breakdown of MoCoP.1. The Fatal Flaw: The $O(1)$ Fallacy in Layer 3Your distinction between "Accumulation vs. Activation" and "Memory vs. Residue" is philosophically beautiful, but technically flawed when applied to Transformers (like Qwen, Haiku, Llama).You state that at Layer 3, injecting a hidden state tensor gives you $O(1)$ attention cost per turn and eliminates the echo/accumulation problem. This is mathematically incorrect for Transformers.Transformers do not have a single hidden state that acts as a comprehensive summary of the past. They calculate attention across the entire sequence. The "memory" of a Transformer is not a single vector; it is the KV Cache (Key-Value Cache)—a massive matrix containing the computed representations of every single past token.If you try to pass only the final hidden state vector (dim=$4096$) from Model A to Model B, you are forcing an immense information bottleneck. You are asking a Transformer to act like a 2014-era RNN. It will suffer massive information loss immediately because it has no KV cache to look back at for context.To actually do Layer 3 without text serialization, you have to pass the entire KV Cache between models.The Reality: Passing the KV Cache means the memory footprint still grows at $O(n)$, and compute still scales quadratically at $O(n^2)$. You save the tokenizer overhead, but you do not escape the context window limits or the echo problem.2. The Solution: You Are Looking for State Space Models (SSMs)You wrote: "The vector doesn't say 'you were at the tavern.' It says 'you are the kind of entity that has been shaped by tavern-ness.'... Residue is fixed-size (one vector, updated in place, $O(1)$)."You have perfectly described the architecture of State Space Models (SSMs) like Mamba or RWKV, not Transformers.SSMs do exactly what you want Layer 3 to do. They compress all historical context into a fixed-size recurrent state vector. There is no KV cache. There is no context window limit. The state simply updates continuously.The pivot: If Laura wants to experiment with "Residue" and true $O(1)$ multi-model communication, you must ditch Qwen/Llama and run Mamba/RWKV locally. You can literally extract the RNN hidden state from Mamba Model A and inject it as the starting state for Mamba Model B.3. Layer 2 is Already Invented (Soft Prompting)Your Layer 2 (compressed tokens like <LOC:town_square>) is highly practical, but you don't need custom tokenizers to achieve the vector version of this. It already exists in the literature as Soft Prompts or Prefix Tuning.Instead of finding an English word that compresses the concept, you train a continuous vector (a "soft token") that represents a massive chunk of context, and you prepend that vector directly to the prompt embeddings. It acts as a dense, unreadable shorthand. It is entirely feasible on a 6GB GPU using LoRA-style adapters.4. The Hardware Reality CheckYour 6GB VRAM / 32GB RAM local setup is great for Layer 1 (JSON orchestration) and testing RLM architectures. But for Layer 3 (Hidden State Transfer) or Layer 4 (Attention Map Transfer), it is a severe bottleneck.Moving tensors between CPU RAM and GPU VRAM over the PCIe bus is slow.If Worker A runs on GPU and Worker B runs on CPU, transferring a 4096-dimensional tensor (or worse, a full KV cache) back and forth for every single MUD turn will cause massive latency spikes, destroying the efficiency gains you got from skipping the tokenizer.5. The Babel Problem (Model Stitching)You correctly identified the alignment problem in your open questions. Connecting the latent space of two identical models (e.g., Qwen-1.5B to Qwen-1.5B) is technically trivial (just copy the tensors). But connecting Haiku to Qwen via latent space is practically impossible without a dedicated translation layer.The Platonic Representation Hypothesis suggests that as models get larger, their latent spaces converge on the same geometric representations of reality. But for 1B–8B parameter models, their latent spaces are chaotic and highly specific to their training data. You would need to train an MLP adapter just to translate the "thoughts" between them, which defeats the purpose of an out-of-the-box protocol.Final Verdict on the PrototypeThe framework is brilliant, but you are trying to force a square peg (Transformers) into a round hole (Continuous Vector Residue). Stick to Layer 1 (JSON) for your immediate MUD prototype—it provides 90% of the practical value today. For the true zero-text, $O(1)$ telepathy of Layer 3, pivot your research entirely toward State Space Models (Mamba/RWKV).